    id: "674a468b27d5b9606c31",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "China sets 2030 targets to boost support for older people, childcare",
    summary: "China is expanding care for both ends of its population – more nursing facilities for a rapidly growing elderly population and more childcare to encourage marriage and births.",
    preview: "China is expanding care for both ends of its population – more nursing facilities for a rapidly growing elderly population and more childcare to encourage marriage and births. BEIJING: China is introducing measures to upgrade care facilities for older people and children by 2030, aiming to adapt to a rapidly aging population and falling birth rates, a government blueprint showed on Monday (Nov 23).",
    body: "China is expanding care for both ends of its population – more nursing facilities for a rapidly growing elderly population and more childcare to encourage marriage and births.\n\nBEIJING: China is introducing measures to upgrade care facilities for older people and children by 2030, aiming to adapt to a rapidly aging population and falling birth rates, a government blueprint showed on Monday (Nov 23).\n\nAuthorities will advance both infrastructure investment and service quality to \"better align the supply of elderly care and childcare services with the needs of the public,\" according to the plan published on the website of the National Development and Reform Commission and jointly guided by the civil affairs, health and education ministries.\n\nBy 2030, nursing care beds will account for 73 per cent of all beds in elderly care institutions, while community care facility coverage for older people will reach 70 per cent and around 2,000 aging public nursing homes will be renovated, the document said.\n\nTo boost childcare capacity, authorities aim to extend childcare service centres across all prefecture-level cities and raise the enrolment rate for children under the age of three by 6 percentage points from 2025 levels.\n\nThe plan also supports kindergartens in renovating or expanding to offer classes for two- to three-year-olds, and encourages employers to use their own premises to provide workplace childcare. Projects will be backed by central budget funds, local government special bonds and commercial credit, alongside tighter supervision of prepaid nursing home fees to curb fraud.\n\nChinese authorities have rolled out a range of measures in recent years to encourage marriage and childbearing, including family subsidies, childcare support and reducing childbirth-related medical costs, but births have continued to fall.\n\nChina's population shrank for a fourth consecutive year in 2025 as its birth rate dropped to a record low, and demographers warn of a similar trend in 2026.\n\nOfficial data showed China's old-age dependency ratio surpassed its child dependency ratio for the first time in 2025, meaning its workforce now supports more older people than children.",
